Layout**\n\nI\'m now implementing the UI elements. I\'ve started structuring `MainActivity.kt` with a `Scaffold` and dark theme. The top section now contains a date and time display, as planned. The central `VoiceInputComponent` is positioned, and I\'m currently working on the grid of four buttons with custom icons and colors, and writing a custom bottom bar. Next, I\'ll tackle the clock and date logic.\n\n**Incorporating Clock/Date**\n\nI\'ve integrated the clock and date logic using `java.time` and `DateTimeFormatter`. It now updates every minute within a `LaunchedEffect`, ensuring the time and date are current. Next, I\'ll implement each of the buttons with the appropriate icons and colors.\n\n\n**Refining the Component**\n\nI\'m now refining the `VoiceInputComponent`. I\'ve verified the speech recognition logic is solid, utilizing the `SpeechRecognizer`. My focus is now on completely overwriting the visual `Box` in `VoiceInputComponent.kt` to match the image\'s style \342\200\223 a central mic icon and text \342\200\223 and then I will move to updating `MainActivity.kt`. I\'m starting by making the `VoiceInputComponent` transparent.\n\n\n**Styling the Voice Input**\n\nI\'m now styling the `VoiceInputComponent` based on the visual specifications. I am creating a blue, glowing circular icon with text below it that changes based on the listening state. It now contains an icon and text, the former of which changes based on the listening state. I\'ll make the visual updates in `VoiceInputComponent.kt` now.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Visual**\n\nI\'m now incorporating a custom wave icon to enhance the visual feedback of the voice input. This involves modifying the existing icon to offer a more dynamic response. It\'s now colored based on the listening state, adding visual interest. Finally, I will integrate the time and date directly into the `MainActivity.kt`.\n\n\n\n**Integrating the Bottom Bar**\n\nI am now integrating the time and date directly into `MainActivity.kt`, to keep it updated with the system time. I\'m also adding a simple bottom bar, using a transparent color scheme, for visual enhancement, and integrating the accessible grid button now.\n\n\n**Adjusting UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now revising the design for the SOS button. I\'m opting for simplicity: if an icon is passed, I\'ll use that; otherwise, text will be displayed. I\'ve also verified the main layout structure in Kotlin, with a `Scaffold` holding a dark background, a time/date display, the `VoiceInputComponent`, and a 2x2 grid. I\'ll need to fill in the grid content next.\n\n\n**Adding the Bottom Bar**\n\nI am now implementing a custom bottom bar that precisely reflects the design in the image. I\'ve configured it with a dark background and incorporated the specified icons: Home, Back, and Mic with the word \"VOICE\" written next to it, matching the exact layout.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Layout**\n\nI\'m now integrating all these UI components. I\'ve focused on adjusting the visual appearance of the bottom bar elements, ensuring the specified blue color for the microphone icon and fine-tuning spacing, to match the target. Now I\'m reviewing the icon size.\n\n\n**Coloring the Buttons**\n\nI\'m now determining the button color scheme. I\'ve set the SOS button to red, the WhatsApp button to green (potentially slightly brighter), and the profile button to blue. I\'ve also decided to integrate the text readout, currently displayed as \"Hai detto: ...\", from debugging, as a subtle UI element, either below the grid or the voice button, to maintain the application\'s accessibility, and I am updating the `VoiceInputComponent.kt` now.\n\n\n"
